this week 48 Hours on a Cold Case murder  that stumped investigators for nearly 35  years Roxanne wood was found dead in her  Michigan home back in 1987. DNA from the  crime scene was preserved but there  wasn't enough evidence to charge any  suspects decades later a genetic  genealogist helped investigators find  the Killer by constructing a family tree  48 Hours correspondent Peter Van Sant  reports  in February 1987 Terry Wood came home  from a night of bowling to discover his  wife Roxanne dead on the kitchen floor  she was bleeding profusely from her neck  detective first lieutenant Chuck  Christensen says Roxanne had been  sexually assaulted and her throat was  slashed  DNA was preserved but given technology  limits at the time not much could be  done with it 34 long years passed  without a real lead I was starting to  lose hope then in 2021 investigative  genetic genealogist Gabriella Vargas got  to work on the Cold Case though only a  scant amount of DNA was left Vargas was  confident I believed that this case was  extremely solvable I believe that I  could solve it incredibly Vargas  identified a suspect in just four days  his name Patrick Gillum but before an  arrest could be made investigators had  to be sure gillum's DNA matched the DNA  left at the scene I'm Ryan cotty and I  am a trooper with the Michigan State  Police Coty went undercover following  Gillum we noticed that he was a smoker  it's a great source of DNA cut and his  team witnessed Gillum flick a cigarette  butt out his window as he drove you're  rubbing your hands you're going this is  going to be that final piece of the  puzzle that you had talked about right  yes they were in for some bad news it  wasn't a match it's possible the butt  picked up on a busy street wasn't the  one that Gillum tossed so Katie followed  him again I saw him throw his cigarette  which was right right in about this area  so it was a great situation because the  cigarette was by itself investigators  held their breath until the results from  this butt came back it was a perfect  match DNA had identified a killer but  could investigators get him to confess  Why didn't it match the first time so I  think because he was following a vehicle  and the it was a busy road it might have  been the wrong cigarette right that's  why he said it was a loan yeah he was  very happy about that catching a little  bag as well so
 

